EXT. FROZEN RESERVOIR - DAWN

The lake lies like a sheet of poured pewter, its far shore dissolved in mist. Snow has drifted in long tongues over the ice. It is entirely silent, the kind of silence with weight.

ODALYS MERCADO (30s, orange parka, a camera strapped across her chest) walks a careful line over the surface, testing each step with an ice pick. Behind her, HANS-PETER VOSS (60s, tall, beard grey with frost, a rucksack twice the size it should be) drags a sled loaded with coiled rope and a sealed metal case.

                    ODALYS
          Look at it. Not a crack.

                    HANS-PETER
          Ice does not announce itself.

                    ODALYS
          You're a cheerful man.

                    HANS-PETER
          I am a careful man.

They walk on. A pale sun clears the ridge and lays copper across the ice. Ahead, a red flag on a bamboo pole marks the survey stake. Odalys quickens. Hans-Peter keeps the sled's rope short, and slow.

She reaches the stake. Photographs it. Photographs the horizon. Laughs at nothing in particular, breath smoking.

                    ODALYS (CONT'D)
          We did it. Day four, and the ice is solid, and --

Under their boots, a long groan rolls out from the center of the lake, a bass note that climbs through the soles of the feet into the teeth. Both freeze. Odalys's smile hangs on her face a second longer than it should.

                    HANS-PETER
          (low)
          Do not run.

                    ODALYS
          I wasn't going to.

                    HANS-PETER
          Your face says you were.

A black line spiders from beneath the sled runner, racing in two directions. Hans-Peter releases the rope. Odalys drops flat, spreading her arms. The sled tilts. The metal case slides, slides, tips, and vanishes into a widening mouth of water without a splash.

They lie side by side, a body's length apart, hands spread on the ice like swimmers on a cold floor.

                    ODALYS
          That was the samples.

                    HANS-PETER
          That was a case.

They crawl. Slowly. The line widens behind them, then stops. The groan fades. Somewhere far off, a raven calls.

Hans-Peter reaches the thick white of the shore snow first, pulls himself onto it, and offers a gloved hand back. Odalys takes it. Pulls up. Both scramble backward from the edge and sit, panting.

                    ODALYS
          Day four.

                    HANS-PETER
          Day four.
